# E4-R12 (N02): stable byte contract for hash-constrained evolution evidence.
#
# The evolution ledger records sha256 OVER RAW FILE BYTES (LF). A fresh
# Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true would rewrite these text files to
# CRLF and break every recorded digest (verified: an all-CRLF read of the
# ledger refs produces 11 DIGEST_MISMATCH). These rules force LF checkout on
# every platform — the storage contract is "git blob bytes", never a silent
# normalization inside the verifier (historical raw-byte digests stay valid).
docs/evolution/*.json text eol=lf
benchmarks/results/**/*.json text eol=lf

# E4-R94 (finding C follow-up): the committed evidence manifests and the frozen
# case selection are read by RAW BYTES. scripts/benchmark/selfcheck-r94-runner.ps1
# section 9 pins the sha256 of three of them, and the R90 raw-byte audit separates
# rawRootDigest (bytes) from rootDigest (normalized content) for exactly this reason.
# Without a rule here, a fresh windows-latest checkout with core.autocrlf=true
# rewrites every one of them to CRLF and the pinned digests no longer match -
# MEASURED: run 35306276313 failed on BOTH windows-latest and ubuntu-latest, because
# that run was the first to execute the R94 selfcheck in CI. The blobs are already
# stored LF (git ls-files --eol reports i/lf for all of them), so this rule changes
# NO committed byte; it only stops the checkout from rewriting them. Same fix and
# same rationale as the two rules above.
docs/evidence/**/*.json text eol=lf

# E4-R84: the campaign evidence fixture is byte-pinned. Its committed root
# digest (asserted in packages/evaluation/src/campaign-validate.test.ts) and its
# generator's `--check` mode both compare bytes, so a Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true must not rewrite it to CRLF.
scripts/benchmark/fixtures/r84-campaign/** text eol=lf

# E4-R84: the CI workflow blob was historically stored WITH CRLF (`git ls-files
# --eol` reported `i/crlf`), so every added line carried a CR and
# `git diff --check` reported it as trailing whitespace — commit 0ca421e
# produced 194 such warnings on this one file. The file is plain-text YAML and
# nothing depends on its stored bytes, so pin it to LF; this is the same fix the
# two rules above already apply to their own files.
.github/workflows/*.yml text eol=lf

# E4-R86: four source files whose git blobs are historically stored WITH CRLF
# (`git ls-files --eol` reports `i/crlf` for each). Every line added to them
# therefore ends in a CR, which `git diff --check` reads as trailing whitespace —
# the same false positive R84 documented above for ci.yml (commit 0ca421e: 194
# warnings on one file). Unlike that file these are TypeScript sources whose real
# change is small, so pinning them to `text eol=lf` would rewrite every line of
# the blob and bury a ~10-line change under a whole-file renormalization.
# Declaring the CR as part of the line ending is byte-only (no blob changes at
# all) and was verified ADDITIVE in a scratch repo: `whitespace=cr-at-eol` still
# reports genuine trailing whitespace and blank-line-at-EOF, for both LF and CRLF
# files. It never suppresses a real whitespace error.
packages/contracts/src/contracts.test.ts whitespace=cr-at-eol
packages/core/src/state/agent-state.test.ts whitespace=cr-at-eol
packages/evaluation/src/manifest.ts whitespace=cr-at-eol
packages/observability/src/metrics.ts whitespace=cr-at-eol